---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import { AUDIT_DATE, AUDIT_DATE_ISO, docsUrl, serverSpec, taglines } from '../content/site';
import { faq } from '../content/faq';
import { faqPage, webPage } from '../lib/schema';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const canonical = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href;

const installFaq = faq.filter((f) =>
  [
    'What do I need to run it?',
    'How long does installing it take?',
    'I am not technical. Can someone set it up for me?',
    'Do backups happen automatically?',
    'What happens if my server cannot reach the licence server?',
  ].includes(f.q),
);
---

<Base
  title="Install a Self-Hosted Client Portal: ClientOS Setup Guide"
  description="What installing ClientOS on your own Linux VPS actually involves: server size, one A record, the one-command installer, browser setup, email and payments, and scheduling backups."
  og="og/install.png"
  crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: `${base}/` }, { label: 'Install guide' }]}
  schema={[
    webPage({
      url: canonical,
      name: 'ClientOS install guide summary',
      description:
        'A summary of what installing a self-hosted ClientOS involves, with links to the official documentation.',
      modified: AUDIT_DATE_ISO,
    }),
    faqPage(installFaq),
  ]}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Install guide</p>
      <h1>What self-hosting actually involves</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A summary of the real work, in order, so you can judge it before you buy anything. The
        official documentation is the authority and every section below links to the page it
        summarises — this page exists to show you the shape of the job, including the parts that are
        work.
      </p>
      <p class="meta" style="margin-top:1.25rem">{taglines[0]}</p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={docsUrl('install_hero')} rel="noopener">
          Official ClientOS docs
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>1. A server</h2>
      <p>
        Any Linux VPS with a public IPv4 address. A fresh one is best, and it must not already be
        running nginx or Apache, because ClientOS brings its own web server and needs ports 80 and
        443. The docs name Hetzner, DigitalOcean, Vultr, Linode and AWS Lightsail; any provider
        works.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <table>
          <caption>
            Server requirements from
            <a href={docsUrl('install_spec', '/installation')} rel="noopener">
              getclientos.com/docs/installation</a>, checked {AUDIT_DATE}.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Resource</th>
              <th scope="col">Minimum</th>
              <th scope="col">Recommended</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              serverSpec.map((s) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{s.resource}</th>
                  <td>{s.min}</td>
                  <td>{s.rec}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="meta">
        The installer checks memory and disk before it starts and stops with a clear message if the
        server is too small; below 2 GB it refuses, and on a small server it adds a 4 GB swap file.
        The docs call a 2 vCPU, 4 GB server "comfortable for a typical agency".
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-raised">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>2. One DNS record, added first</h2>
      <p>
        ClientOS runs on <strong>one domain</strong> — say <code>clients.youragency.com</code> — with
        the client portal at <code>/</code>, the admin at <code>/admin</code> and the API at
        <code>/v1</code>. It needs a single <strong>A record</strong> pointing that name at your
        server’s IP address.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`Type   Name      Value          TTL
A      clients   203.0.113.5    Auto (or 300)`}</code></pre>
      <p>Three things the docs are specific about, each of which will otherwise cost you an hour:</p>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>
          <strong>Add it before you install.</strong> The installer checks the record against public
          DNS and against your actual server before it downloads anything.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On Cloudflare, set it to DNS only</strong> — the grey cloud. Let’s Encrypt has to
          reach your server directly for the first certificate, and the orange-cloud proxy blocks
          that. You can turn the proxy on afterwards.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Delete a stale AAAA record</strong> on that name unless it is your server’s own
          IPv6 address. Let’s Encrypt tries IPv6 first, and a wrong AAAA record stops the
          certificate.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The domain has to be public, must not already be serving a website, and <strong
          >your licence binds to it</strong
        > — a key can change domain three times in its life, so pick the one you mean to keep.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={docsUrl('install_dns', '/domain')} rel="noopener">
          Domain and DNS docs
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>3. One command</h2>
      <p>
        Your licence page at vibemonk.com gives you an install command with your key already in it.
        SSH into the server, paste it, press enter.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`$ ssh root@YOUR_SERVER_IP
$ curl -fsSL https://license.vibemonk.com/v1/licensing/install/clientos \\
    | sudo bash -s -- --license-key CLOS-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX`}</code></pre>
      <p>It checks your key, downloads the installer and verifies its fingerprint, unpacks it to
        <code>/opt/clientos</code>, installs Docker if it is missing, adds swap on a small server,
        and writes your configuration with freshly generated passwords. Then it prints a link to
        finish in the browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you would rather not pipe a script into bash, the same installer is downloadable as a
        <code>.tar.gz</code> from your licence page — upload it, unpack it, run
        <code>sudo ./install.sh --license-key …</code>. Both paths run exactly the same checks. For a
        provisioning script, add <code>--domain</code>, <code>--email</code> and <code>--yes</code> to
        skip the browser step without skipping the checks.
      </p>
      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <strong>One constraint worth knowing early:</strong> ClientOS installs only on a server
          your domain reaches over the internet. Before anything downloads, the licence server
          checks from outside that your domain answers on that machine — so a laptop, a home network
          or an unpointed domain cannot install it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-raised">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>4. Finish in the browser</h2>
      <p>
        Open the link the terminal printed, enter your domain and an email address for certificate
        renewal warnings, and press Connect. The licence server verifies your domain from the
        internet and binds your key to it; only then is ClientOS downloaded, with every file checked
        against its fingerprint. HTTPS takes a minute or two, then your browser moves on to the setup
        page by itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        The setup page at <code>/admin/setup</code> is guarded by a <strong>setup key</strong> the
        installer printed — it stops a stranger who finds your fresh server from claiming it first.
        If you lose it, <code>sudo clientos setup-key</code> shows it again. Seven steps: welcome,
        licence, your agency, region (currency and timezone), two brand colours, your owner account
        (password at least 12 characters), review. About two minutes.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">
        Once anyone on your team has signed in, setup closes for good. There is also a command-line
        route to create the owner, which refuses to run after any staff member has signed in, so it
        cannot be used as a back door later.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={docsUrl('install_firstrun', '/first-run')} rel="noopener">
          First-run docs
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>5. Connect email, then everything else</h2>
      <p>
        This is where the time actually goes. The docs put first run at 20 to 40 minutes, most of it
        spent in your email provider’s dashboard adding SPF and DKIM records, and they give the order
        for a reason:
      </p>
      <ol class="steps" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <li>
          <span class="step-n">01</span>
          <h3>Email, first</h3>
          <p>
            Resend, or any SMTP server — Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, Zoho, Amazon SES. There is
            no fallback: until one is connected, sign-in codes, invitations and notifications cannot
            be sent.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-n">02</span>
          <h3>Sender and a test</h3>
          <p>
            Set the sender name and an address on the domain you verified, then send a test to your
            own inbox before you trust it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-n">03</span>
          <h3>File storage</h3>
          <p>
            Already working on your server. If you would rather keep files in a Cloudflare R2 bucket,
            decide now — before anything is uploaded — because existing files have to be copied over
            by hand.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-n">04</span>
          <h3>Turnstile</h3>
          <p>
            Optional but recommended. Cloudflare Turnstile protects your public signup and free
            checkout pages from bots.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-n">05</span>
          <h3>Payments, test keys first</h3>
          <p>
            Connect Stripe, PayPal, NOWPayments, CoinGate or BTCPay Server with test keys, make a
            test purchase, confirm the order and the webhook arrived — then switch to live keys and
            repeat with a small real payment you refund.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-n">06</span>
          <h3>Two-factor, then your team</h3>
          <p>
            Turn on 2FA for your own account. Only then invite anyone: an invitation sent before
            email works never arrives.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-raised">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>6. Backups — the part you have to do yourself</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        If you read one section of this page, read this one. ClientOS gives you a one-command backup,
        but the docs are explicit that <strong>nothing runs it until you schedule it</strong>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`$ sudo clientos backup
# writes three files into backups/ and keeps the newest seven of each:
#   db-DATE.dump       your whole database
#   files-DATE.tar.gz  every file stored on the server
#   env-DATE           deploy/.env, including CREDENTIAL_SECRET

# then schedule it — daily at 03:00:
$ echo '0 3 * * * root /usr/local/bin/clientos backup >/var/log/clientos-backup.log 2>&1' \\
    | sudo tee /etc/cron.d/clientos-backup`}</code></pre>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>
          <strong>Copy the folder off the server.</strong> A backup on the same disk is lost with the
          disk. Another server, your own machine, or a bucket via rclone — and keep the copies
          private, because the <code>env</code> files hold your secrets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Restore one before your first real client goes in</strong>, and again every few
          months. The docs put it well: a backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check that recent files exist.</strong> A backup job that silently stopped weeks
          ago is the most common failure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If you move files to Cloudflare R2</strong>, <code>clientos backup</code> no longer
          covers them and the bucket needs backing up separately.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Living with it: the clientos command</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <table>
          <caption>
            From
            <a href={docsUrl('install_ops', '/operations')} rel="noopener">
              getclientos.com/docs/operations</a>, checked {AUDIT_DATE}. Run with sudo.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos status</code></th><td>The version, what is running and whether it is healthy</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos update</code></th><td>Takes a backup, downloads the newest release your licence covers, restarts. Migrations run before the new API starts</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos rollback</code></th><td>Back to the release before the last update, once</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos backup</code></th><td>Database, files and settings into <code>backups/</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos restore FILE…</code></th><td>Puts a backup back; asks you to type <code>restore</code> to confirm</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos logs [service]</code></th><td>Follows the logs, of everything or one service</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos domain NEW</code></th><td>Moves ClientOS, and your licence, to another domain</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>clientos setup-key</code></th><td>Shows the setup page’s key again</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Updates also run from the admin at Settings → License → Updates, and the docs note that the
        admin never gets root access for this — it leaves a request that a small service on the
        server picks up. A release that changes existing data will say so in its notes and wants
        <code>clientos update --stop-first</code>, which costs about a minute of downtime instead of
        the old version writing old-style data into the new database.
      </p>
      <p>
        Point an uptime monitor at <code>https://YOUR_DOMAIN/v1/health</code>: it returns 200 with
        PostgreSQL and Redis reported, and 503 when the database cannot be reached. Docker restarts a
        container whose process crashes, but not one that is running and unhealthy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-raised">
    <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
      <h2>Install questions</h2>
      <FaqList items={installFaq} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <strong>This page is a summary, written {AUDIT_DATE}.</strong> The
          <a href={docsUrl('install_footer')} rel="noopener">official ClientOS documentation</a> is
          the authority and is kept current — follow it, not this, when you actually install. If the
          two ever disagree, the docs are right and this page is out of date; tell us in a
          <a href="https://github.com/huelab-byte/tryclientos/issues" rel="noopener">GitHub issue</a>
          and we will fix it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    utm="install"
    heading="Or skip the server and just look at it"
    body="The instant demo emails you a login to a live, fully installed ClientOS with sample clients, orders and invoices. Read-only, 3 days, no call and no card."
  />
</Base>
